From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: wasm64 port <wasm64@localhost>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 08:18:30 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] wasm64 port: Lean 4.34.0 / Mathlib v4.34.0; one syntax for the shared tactics
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

- Game.lean: Languages "English" -> "en" (ISO code the client can match).
- MathlibTheorems: drop the vestigial `import Mathlib.Tactic.Polyrith`
  (not in the essential Mathlib pack; polyrith needs an external Sage
  oracle and no level, hint or doc uses it).
- MathlibTheorems: `import Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic` ->
  `import Mathlib.Basic.Real.Basic` (Mathlib v4.34.0 keeps the old name only
  as a deprecated_module shim for this module, which would warn).
- LevelLemmas/settheory, settheory_knightsknavesfoundation: keep the
  `[DecidableEq _]` binder on the `variable` line -- the newer parser ends
  a command at a column-0 token, which orphaned the binder and lost every
  Finset `Inter`/`Insert` instance in these files.
- LevelLemmas/settheory_KnightsAndKnaves, settheory_KnightsAndKnaves3:
  `set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency false in` before the two
  `inductive ... deriving DecidableEq, Fintype` enums (under Lean 4.34's
  stricter defeq check the derived Fintype instance's `rewrite` fails;
  Mathlib sets the same option at its own call sites).
- SetTheory_Knights_Knaves L09: remove `simp at diff` from the model proof
  (no progress on this Mathlib; the following steps reach the same state).
- New LevelLemmas/knightsknaves_tactic_syntax (2026-10-23): the syntax of
  `knight_or_knave`, `knight_interp` and `knave_interp`, declared once;
  dsl_KnightsAndKnaves and settheory_knightsknavesfoundation import it and
  keep their expansions as `macro_rules` instead of each declaring the
  syntax with `macro`. The wasm64 build checks every level in one
  environment holding the whole game, where the two worlds' `macro`s gave
  the parser two syntaxes for the same text and Lean ran only the first
  (the set-theory one): 13 DSL_Knights_Knaves levels failed with their own
  model proofs. With one syntax Lean tries both expansions and keeps the
  one that works, as it already does for both worlds' `contradiction`
  extensions. A level that imports only its own world is unchanged.

No level or world removed; nothing a player types changes.

